itualistic behavior** — hoarding, repetitive movements; (5) **Hyperorality and dietary changes** — craving sweets, binge eating, attempting to eat inedible objects; (6) **Neuropsychological profile** showing **executive dysfunction with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial function**. Brain MRI shows **frontal and/or anterior temporal** atrophy, often asymmetric. FTD typically has an earlier onset (45-65 years) compared to AD (>65). Treatment is supportive: SSRIs for disinhibition and compulsive behaviors, trazodone for agitation. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Rascovsky Criteria 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old male is brought to the clinic by his spouse due to striking behavioral changes over the past 3 years. The spouse reports that the patient has become increasingly socially inappropriate (making crude comments to strangers), shows a profound lack of empathy, has developed a compulsive craving for sweets (**hyperorality**), and repeatedly hoards items. Memory and visuospatial skills remain relatively intact on cognitive testing. Brain MRI reveals **predominantly left frontal and temporal** atrophy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD) \u2014 characterized by early personality and behavioral changes (disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, hyperorality, compulsive behaviors) with relative preservation of memory and visuospatial skills; caused by frontal and anterior temporal lobe degeneration", "Obsessive-compulsive disorder \u2014 characterized by ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions that the patient recognizes as excessive", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 characterized by early and prominent episodic memory loss with preservation of social behavior until late stages", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies \u2014 characterized by fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism", "Bipolar disorder (manic episode) \u2014 characterized by elevated mood, pressured speech, decreased need for sleep, and grandiosity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient''''s presentation is classic for **behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD)**, the most common subtype of FTD. Diagnostic criteria (Rascovsky criteria) require at least 3 of the following 6 features: (1) **Early behavioral disinhibition** — socially inappropriate behavior, loss of manners; (2) **Early apathy/inertia** — loss of motivation, reduced initiative; (3) **Early loss of sympathy/empathy** — diminished response to others'''' needs and feelings; (4) **Early perseverative, stereotyped, or compulsive/ritualistic behavior** — hoarding, repetitive movements; (5) **Hyperorality and dietary changes** — craving sweets, binge eating, attempting to eat inedible objects; (6) **Neuropsychological profile** showing **executive dysfunction with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial function**. Brain MRI shows **frontal and/or anterior temporal** atrophy, often asymmetric. FTD typically has an earlier onset (45-65 years) compared to AD (>65). Treatment is supportive: SSRIs for disinhibition and compulsive behaviors, trazodone for agitation. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Rascovsky Criteria 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male presents with a 2-year history of progressive difficulty understanding the meaning of words and recognizing objects. The patient speaks fluently with normal grammar and articulation but frequently asks, ''''What does that word mean?'''' and cannot name common objects (e.g., calls a ''''hippopotamus'''' an ''''animal thing''''). Episodic memory, visuospatial skills, and behavior are well preserved. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy**, predominantly on the left side. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA) \u2014 a subtype of frontotemporal dementia characterized by progressive loss of semantic knowledge (word meaning and object recognition) with preserved fluency, grammar, and speech production", "Broca''''s aphasia from a left frontal stroke \u2014 characterized by nonfluent, effortful speech with agrammatism and preserved comprehension", "Behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia \u2014 characterized by personality changes, disinhibition, and hyperorality rather than language deficits", "Wernicke''''s aphasia from a left temporal stroke \u2014 characterized by fluent but nonsensical (jargon) speech with impaired comprehension and absent awareness of deficits", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 characterized by early episodic memory loss rather than selective loss of semantic knowledge"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA)** is a subtype of FTD-spectrum disease characterized by **progressive loss of semantic knowledge** — the meanings of words and concepts. Key features include: (1) **Impaired confrontation naming** — cannot name objects, uses circumlocutions (''''the thing you write with'''' instead of ''''pen''''); (2) **Impaired single-word comprehension** — asks ''''what does that mean?'''' for previously known words; (3) **Surface dyslexia/dysgraphia** — reads/writes words by rules rather than by sight (e.g., reads ''''yacht'''' as ''''yatched''''); (4) **Preserved speech fluency and grammar** — sentences are grammatically correct but semantically empty; (5) **Preserved episodic memory and visuospatial skills** early on. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy** (knife-edge atrophy of the temporal poles), typically left-predominant. Pathologically, svPPA is most commonly associated with **TDP-43 Type C** inclusions. No disease-modifying treatments exist; management is supportive with speech-language therapy. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Gorno-Tempini Classification 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 59-year-old man presents with a 2-year history of progressive difficulty understanding the meaning of words and recognizing objects. The patient speaks fluently with normal grammar and articulation but frequently asks, ''''What does that word mean?'''' and cannot name common objects (e.g., calls a ''''hippopotamus'''' an ''''animal thing''''). Episodic memory, visuospatial skills, and behavior are well preserved. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy**, predominantly on the left side. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA) \u2014 a subtype of frontotemporal dementia characterized by progressive loss of semantic knowledge (word meaning and object recognition) with preserved fluency, grammar, and speech production", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 characterized by early episodic memory loss rather than selective loss of semantic knowledge", "Broca''''s aphasia from a left frontal stroke \u2014 characterized by nonfluent, effortful speech with agrammatism and preserved comprehension", "Wernicke''''s aphasia from a left temporal stroke \u2014 characterized by fluent but nonsensical (jargon) speech with impaired comprehension and absent awareness of deficits", "Behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia \u2014 characterized by personality changes, disinhibition, and hyperorality rather than language deficits"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA)** is a subtype of FTD-spectrum disease characterized by **progressive loss of semantic knowledge** — the meanings of words and concepts. Key features include: (1) **Impaired confrontation naming** — cannot name objects, uses circumlocutions (''''the thing you write with'''' instead of ''''pen''''); (2) **Impaired single-word comprehension** — asks ''''what does that mean?'''' for previously known words; (3) **Surface dyslexia/dysgraphia** — reads/writes words by rules rather than by sight (e.g., reads ''''yacht'''' as ''''yatched''''); (4) **Preserved speech fluency and grammar** — sentences are grammatically correct but semantically empty; (5) **Preserved episodic memory and visuospatial skills** early on. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy** (knife-edge atrophy of the temporal poles), typically left-predominant. Pathologically, svPPA is most commonly associated with **TDP-43 Type C** inclusions. No disease-modifying treatments exist; management is supportive with speech-language therapy. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Gorno-Tempini Classification 2011)'),
