    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old male presents with a 4-year history of progressive difficulty understanding the meaning of words and recognizing objects. The patient speaks fluently with normal grammar and articulation but frequently asks, ''''What does that word mean?'''' and cannot name common objects (e.g., calls a ''''hippopotamus'''' an ''''animal thing''''). Episodic memory, visuospatial skills, and behavior are well preserved. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy**, predominantly on the left side. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA) \u2014 a subtype of frontotemporal dementia characterized by progressive loss of semantic knowledge (word meaning and object recognition) with preserved fluency, grammar, and speech production", "Wernicke''''s aphasia from a left temporal stroke \u2014 characterized by fluent but nonsensical (jargon) speech with impaired comprehension and absent awareness of deficits", "Behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia \u2014 characterized by personality changes, disinhibition, and hyperorality rather than language deficits", "Broca''''s aphasia from a left frontal stroke \u2014 characterized by nonfluent, effortful speech with agrammatism and preserved comprehension", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 characterized by early episodic memory loss rather than selective loss of semantic knowledge"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Semantic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (svPPA)** is a subtype of FTD-spectrum disease characterized by **progressive loss of semantic knowledge** — the meanings of words and concepts. Key features include: (1) **Impaired confrontation naming** — cannot name objects, uses circumlocutions (''''the thing you write with'''' instead of ''''pen''''); (2) **Impaired single-word comprehension** — asks ''''what does that mean?'''' for previously known words; (3) **Surface dyslexia/dysgraphia** — reads/writes words by rules rather than by sight (e.g., reads ''''yacht'''' as ''''yatched''''); (4) **Preserved speech fluency and grammar** — sentences are grammatically correct but semantically empty; (5) **Preserved episodic memory and visuospatial skills** early on. Brain MRI shows **asymmetric anterior temporal lobe atrophy** (knife-edge atrophy of the temporal poles), typically left-predominant. Pathologically, svPPA is most commonly associated with **TDP-43 Type C** inclusions. No disease-modifying treatments exist; management is supportive with speech-language therapy. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Gorno-Tempini Classification 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old male presents with a 5-year history of progressively worsening speech output. The patient speaks with great effort, producing short, grammatically simplified sentences (e.g., ''''Me... go... store... yesterday'''' instead of ''''I went to the store yesterday''''). Comprehension of simple sentences is preserved, but the patient struggles with syntactically complex sentences. Speech is halting with sound distortions (apraxia of speech). Brain MRI shows **left posterior frontal and insular atrophy**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Logopenic variant PPA \u2014 characterized by word-finding pauses in fluent speech with preserved grammar and impaired sentence repetition", "Behavioral variant FTD \u2014 characterized by personality changes and behavioral disinhibition rather than speech production deficits", "Broca''''s aphasia from acute ischemic stroke \u2014 this diagnosis requires sudden onset rather than progressive worsening over years", "Semantic variant PPA \u2014 characterized by loss of word meaning with preserved fluency and grammar", "Nonfluent/agrammatic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (nfvPPA) \u2014 a subtype of frontotemporal dementia characterized by effortful, halting speech production with agrammatism and/or apraxia of speech, with left posterior frontal/insular atrophy"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA (nfvPPA)** is characterized by: (1) **Agrammatism in language production** — simplified grammar, omission of function words and verb inflections, shortened sentence length; and/or (2) **Apraxia of speech** — effortful, halting speech with inconsistent sound errors and distortions, impaired speech motor planning. Comprehension of single words is preserved, but understanding of **syntactically complex sentences** is impaired (e.g., ''''The dog that the cat chased was brown''''). Brain MRI shows **left posterior frontal (inferior frontal gyrus/Broca''''s area) and insular cortex atrophy**. Pathologically, nfvPPA is most commonly associated with **tau-positive pathology** (4R-tau, corticobasal degeneration or progressive supranuclear palsy pathology). Importantly, nfvPPA differs from acute Broca''''s aphasia (stroke) by its **insidious, progressive** course. Treatment is speech-language therapy focused on compensatory communication strategies. (Ref: Gorno-Tempini Classification 2011; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
