 atrophy**. Pathologically, nfvPPA is most commonly associated with **tau-positive pathology** (4R-tau, corticobasal degeneration or progressive supranuclear palsy pathology). Importantly, nfvPPA differs from acute Broca''''s aphasia (stroke) by its **insidious, progressive** course. Treatment is speech-language therapy focused on compensatory communication strategies. (Ref: Gorno-Tempini Classification 2011; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
