    (v_topic_id, 'A 61-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of progressively worsening speech output. The patient speaks with great effort, producing short, grammatically simplified sentences (e.g., ''''Me... go... store... yesterday'''' instead of ''''I went to the store yesterday''''). Comprehension of simple sentences is preserved, but the patient struggles with syntactically complex sentences. Speech is halting with sound distortions (apraxia of speech). Brain MRI shows **left posterior frontal and insular atrophy**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Nonfluent/agrammatic variant Primary Progressive Aphasia (nfvPPA) \u2014 a subtype of frontotemporal dementia characterized by effortful, halting speech production with agrammatism and/or apraxia of speech, with left posterior frontal/insular atrophy", "Behavioral variant FTD \u2014 characterized by personality changes and behavioral disinhibition rather than speech production deficits", "Logopenic variant PPA \u2014 characterized by word-finding pauses in fluent speech with preserved grammar and impaired sentence repetition", "Broca''''s aphasia from acute ischemic stroke \u2014 this diagnosis requires sudden onset rather than progressive worsening over years", "Semantic variant PPA \u2014 characterized by loss of word meaning with preserved fluency and grammar"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA (nfvPPA)** is characterized by: (1) **Agrammatism in language production** — simplified grammar, omission of function words and verb inflections, shortened sentence length; and/or (2) **Apraxia of speech** — effortful, halting speech with inconsistent sound errors and distortions, impaired speech motor planning. Comprehension of single words is preserved, but understanding of **syntactically complex sentences** is impaired (e.g., ''''The dog that the cat chased was brown''''). Brain MRI shows **left posterior frontal (inferior frontal gyrus/Broca''''s area) and insular cortex at