    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old man with behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia has a family history of his father dying of ALS at age 55 and a paternal aunt developing FTD at age 52. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following genetic findings is MOST likely in this family?', '["C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the most common genetic cause of both familial FTD and familial ALS, explaining the co-occurrence of both diseases in this family through a shared genetic mechanism", "NOTCH3 mutation on chromosome 19 \u2014 causing hereditary frontotemporal degeneration with subcortical infarcts", "Presenilin-1 (PSEN1) mutation on chromosome 14 \u2014 the most common cause of familial FTD with motor neuron disease", "Huntingtin (HTT) gene trinucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the primary cause of behavioral FTD in families with co-occurring movement disorders", "APOE \u03b54 homozygosity \u2014 the primary genetic cause of all frontotemporal dementia subtypes, regardless of family history"]'::jsonb, 0, '**C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion** (GGGGCC repeat in the C9orf72 gene on chromosome 9p21) is the **most common genetic cause of BOTH familial FTD (~25%) and familial ALS (~40%)**. It explains the well-recognized **FTD-ALS spectrum** — families may have members with FTD, ALS, or both (FTD-ALS overlap). The three major FTD genes are: (1) **C9orf72** — most common, autosomal dominant, associated with bvFTD, FTD-ALS, and psychotic features; (2) **MAPT** (microtubule-associated protein tau, chromosome 17) — causes tau-positive FTD (formerly FTDP-17); (3) **GRN** (progranulin, chromosome 17) — causes TDP-43-positive FTD, often with asymmetric atrophy. APOE ε4 is an AD risk factor, not FTD. PSEN1 causes early-onset AD. HTT causes Huntington''''s disease. NOTCH3 causes CADASIL. Genetic counseling is essential before testing. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old man with behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia has a family history of his father dying of ALS at age 55 and a paternal aunt developing FTD at age 52. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following genetic findings is MOST likely in this family?', '["APOE \u03b54 homozygosity \u2014 the primary genetic cause of all frontotemporal dementia subtypes, regardless of family history", "Huntingtin (HTT) gene trinucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the primary cause of behavioral FTD in families with co-occurring movement disorders", "C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the most common genetic cause of both familial FTD and familial ALS, explaining the co-occurrence of both diseases in this family through a shared genetic mechanism", "Presenilin-1 (PSEN1) mutation on chromosome 14 \u2014 the most common cause of familial FTD with motor neuron disease", "NOTCH3 mutation on chromosome 19 \u2014 causing hereditary frontotemporal degeneration with subcortical infarcts"]'::jsonb, 2, '**C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion** (GGGGCC repeat in the C9orf72 gene on chromosome 9p21) is the **most common genetic cause of BOTH familial FTD (~25%) and familial ALS (~40%)**. It explains the well-recognized **FTD-ALS spectrum** — families may have members with FTD, ALS, or both (FTD-ALS overlap). The three major FTD genes are: (1) **C9orf72** — most common, autosomal dominant, associated with bvFTD, FTD-ALS, and psychotic features; (2) **MAPT** (microtubule-associated protein tau, chromosome 17) — causes tau-positive FTD (formerly FTDP-17); (3) **GRN** (progranulin, chromosome 17) — causes TDP-43-positive FTD, often with asymmetric atrophy. APOE ε4 is an AD risk factor, not FTD. PSEN1 causes early-onset AD. HTT causes Huntington''''s disease. NOTCH3 causes CADASIL. Genetic counseling is essential before testing. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
