    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old female with behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia has a family history of her father dying of ALS at age 55 and a paternal aunt developing FTD at age 52. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following genetic findings is MOST likely in this family?', '["Presenilin-1 (PSEN1) mutation on chromosome 14 \u2014 the most common cause of familial FTD with motor neuron disease", "Huntingtin (HTT) gene trinucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the primary cause of behavioral FTD in families with co-occurring movement disorders", "C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion \u2014 the most common genetic cause of both familial FTD and familial ALS, explaining the co-occurrence of both diseases in this family through a shared genetic mechanism", "APOE \u03b54 homozygosity \u2014 the primary genetic cause of all frontotemporal dementia subtypes, regardless of family history", "NOTCH3 mutation on chromosome 19 \u2014 causing hereditary frontotemporal degeneration with subcortical infarcts"]'::jsonb, 2, '**C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion** (GGGGCC repeat in the C9orf72 gene on chromosome 9p21) is the **most common genetic cause of BOTH familial FTD (~25%) and familial ALS (~40%)**. It explains the well-recognized **FTD-ALS spectrum** — families may have members with FTD, ALS, or both (FTD-ALS overlap). The three major FTD genes are: (1) **C9orf72** — most common, autosomal dominant, associated with bvFTD, FTD-ALS, and psychotic features; (2) **MAPT** (microtubule-associated protein tau, chromosome 17) — causes tau-positive FTD (formerly FTDP-17); (3) **GRN** (progranulin, chromosome 17) — causes TDP-43-positive FTD, often with asymmetric atrophy. APOE ε4 is an AD risk factor, not FTD. PSEN1 causes early-onset AD. HTT causes Huntington''''s disease. NOTCH3 causes CADASIL. Genetic counseling is essential before testing. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A family brings their 67-year-old relative to the clinic because of behavioral changes and cognitive decline. They want to know whether the condition is Alzheimer''''s disease or frontotemporal dementia. Which of the following features is MOST suggestive of **frontotemporal dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Younger age of onset (typically 45-65 years), early personality and behavioral changes (disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy) as the presenting symptom BEFORE significant memory loss, with frontal and anterior temporal lobe atrophy on MRI", "Rapidly progressive dementia over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Acute stepwise cognitive decline temporally related to cerebrovascular events, with focal neurological deficits and multiple infarcts on brain MRI", "Fluctuating cognition with pronounced day-to-day variation, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism with resting tremor", "Onset after age 75, insidious progressive amnesia as the first and most prominent symptom, with bilateral hippocampal and temporoparietal atrophy on MRI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Key features distinguishing **FTD from AD**: (1) **Age of onset**: FTD typically 45-65 years (the most common dementia in patients <60); AD typically >65 years; (2) **Presenting symptoms**: FTD — **behavioral/personality changes FIRST** (disinhibition, apathy, empathy loss, hyperorality) OR **language deficits first** (PPA variants); AD — **memory loss FIRST** (episodic memory, delayed recall); (3) **Cognitive profile**: FTD — executive dysfunction with **relative preservation of memory** and visuospatial function early on; AD — prominent memory and visuospatial impairment; (4) **Neuroimaging**: FTD — **frontal and/or anterior temporal** atrophy, often asymmetric; AD — **hippocampal and temporoparietal** atrophy; (5) **Insight**: FTD patients often have **poor insight** into their behavioral changes; AD patients may have early awareness of memory problems; (6) **Behavior**: FTD — disinhibition, compulsions, dietary changes; AD — typically preserved social behavior until moderate-severe stages. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
