    (v_topic_id, 'A family brings their 52-year-old relative to the clinic because of behavioral changes and cognitive decline. They want to know whether the condition is Alzheimer''''s disease or frontotemporal dementia. Which of the following features is MOST suggestive of **frontotemporal dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Fluctuating cognition with pronounced day-to-day variation, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism with resting tremor", "Rapidly progressive dementia over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Onset after age 75, insidious progressive amnesia as the first and most prominent symptom, with bilateral hippocampal and temporoparietal atrophy on MRI", "Acute stepwise cognitive decline temporally related to cerebrovascular events, with focal neurological deficits and multiple infarcts on brain MRI", "Younger age of onset (typically 45-65 years), early personality and behavioral changes (disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy) as the presenting symptom BEFORE significant memory loss, with frontal and anterior temporal lobe atrophy on MRI"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Key features distinguishing **FTD from AD**: (1) **Age of onset**: FTD typically 45-65 years (the most common dementia in patients <60); AD typically >65 years; (2) **Presenting symptoms**: FTD — **behavioral/personality changes FIRST** (disinhibition, apathy, empathy loss, hyperorality) OR **language deficits first** (PPA variants); AD — **memory loss FIRST** (episodic memory, delayed recall); (3) **Cognitive profile**: FTD — executive dysfunction with **relative preservation of memory** and visuospatial function early on; AD — prominent memory and visuospatial impairment; (4) **Neuroimaging**: FTD — **frontal and/or anterior temporal** atrophy, often asymmetric; AD — **hippocampal and temporoparietal** atrophy; (5) **Insight**: FTD patients often have **poor insight** into their behavioral changes; AD patients may have early awareness of memory problems; (6) **Behavior**: FTD — disinhibition, compulsions, dietary changes; AD — typically preserved social behavior until moderate-severe stages. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 52-year-old male with a 3-year history of behavioral changes (apathy, disinhibition, poor hygiene) is now developing progressive muscle weakness, fasciculations in the upper extremities, hyperreflexia, and difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). EMG shows evidence of widespread denervation with fibrillation potentials. Which of the following is the most likely unifying diagnosis?', '["FTD-ALS (Frontotemporal Dementia with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis) \u2014 a recognized overlap syndrome where behavioral/cognitive features of FTD co-occur with upper and lower motor neuron signs of ALS, most commonly associated with C9orf72 mutations", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 prion disease causing both cognitive decline and motor neuron degeneration within weeks", "Parkinson''''s disease \u2014 causing both cognitive decline and progressive motor weakness with fasciculations", "Alzheimer''''s disease with coincidental cervical myelopathy \u2014 the motor findings are unrelated to the cognitive decline", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease causing both cognitive decline and mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs"]'::jsonb, 0, '**FTD-ALS** is a well-recognized overlap syndrome in which features of **frontotemporal dementia** (behavioral changes, executive dysfunction, language impairment) co-occur with **amyotrophic lateral sclerosis** (upper motor neuron signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Babinski; AND lower motor neuron signs: muscle weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillation potentials on EMG). Key points: (1) **~15% of FTD patients** develop motor neuron disease; **~50% of ALS patients** develop some degree of cognitive/behavioral impairment; (2) The most common genetic cause is **C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion**; (3) When both FTD and ALS features are present, the prognosis is significantly worse (median survival 2-3 years) compared to either condition alone; (4) Pathologically, most FTD-ALS cases show **TDP-43** inclusions. EMG findings (widespread denervation, fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) confirm lower motor neuron involvement. Bulbar symptoms (dysphagia, dysarthria) are common and may necessitate early advance care planning and discussion of PEG tube placement. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 54-year-old woman with a 2-year history of behavioral changes (apathy, disinhibition, poor hygiene) is now developing progressive muscle weakness, fasciculations in the upper extremities, hyperreflexia, and difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). EMG shows evidence of widespread denervation with fibrillation potentials. Which of the following is the most likely unifying diagnosis?', '["Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease causing both cognitive decline and mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 prion disease causing both cognitive decline and motor neuron degeneration within weeks", "Alzheimer''''s disease with coincidental cervical myelopathy \u2014 the motor findings are unrelated to the cognitive decline", "FTD-ALS (Frontotemporal Dementia with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis) \u2014 a recognized overlap syndrome where behavioral/cognitive features of FTD co-occur with upper and lower motor neuron signs of ALS, most commonly associated with C9orf72 mutations", "Parkinson''''s disease \u2014 causing both cognitive decline and progressive motor weakness with fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 3, '**FTD-ALS** is a well-recognized overlap syndrome in which features of **frontotemporal dementia** (behavioral changes, executive dysfunction, language impairment) co-occur with **amyotrophic lateral sclerosis** (upper motor neuron signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Babinski; AND lower motor neuron signs: muscle weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillation potentials on EMG). Key points: (1) **~15% of FTD patients** develop motor neuron disease; **~50% of ALS patients** develop some degree of cognitive/behavioral impairment; (2) The most common genetic cause is **C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion**; (3) When both FTD and ALS features are present, the prognosis is significantly worse (median survival 2-3 years) compared to either condition alone; (4) Pathologically, most FTD-ALS cases show **TDP-43** inclusions. EMG findings (widespread denervation, fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) confirm lower motor neuron involvement. Bulbar symptoms (dysphagia, dysarthria) are common and may necessitate early advance care planning and discussion of PEG tube placement. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
