    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old man with a 5-year history of behavioral changes (apathy, disinhibition, poor hygiene) is now developing progressive muscle weakness, fasciculations in the upper extremities, hyperreflexia, and difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). EMG shows evidence of widespread denervation with fibrillation potentials. Which of the following is the most likely unifying diagnosis?', '["FTD-ALS (Frontotemporal Dementia with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis) \u2014 a recognized overlap syndrome where behavioral/cognitive features of FTD co-occur with upper and lower motor neuron signs of ALS, most commonly associated with C9orf72 mutations", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 prion disease causing both cognitive decline and motor neuron degeneration within weeks", "Alzheimer''''s disease with coincidental cervical myelopathy \u2014 the motor findings are unrelated to the cognitive decline", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease causing both cognitive decline and mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs", "Parkinson''''s disease \u2014 causing both cognitive decline and progressive motor weakness with fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 0, '**FTD-ALS** is a well-recognized overlap syndrome in which features of **frontotemporal dementia** (behavioral changes, executive dysfunction, language impairment) co-occur with **amyotrophic lateral sclerosis** (upper motor neuron signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Babinski; AND lower motor neuron signs: muscle weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillation potentials on EMG). Key points: (1) **~15% of FTD patients** develop motor neuron disease; **~50% of ALS patients** develop some degree of cognitive/behavioral impairment; (2) The most common genetic cause is **C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion**; (3) When both FTD and ALS features are present, the prognosis is significantly worse (median survival 2-3 years) compared to either condition alone; (4) Pathologically, most FTD-ALS cases show **TDP-43** inclusions. EMG findings (widespread denervation, fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) confirm lower motor neuron involvement. Bulbar symptoms (dysphagia, dysarthria) are common and may necessitate early advance care planning and discussion of PEG tube placement. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
