    (v_topic_id, 'A 63-year-old woman with a 5-year history of behavioral changes (apathy, disinhibition, poor hygiene) is now developing progressive muscle weakness, fasciculations in the upper extremities, hyperreflexia, and difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). EMG shows evidence of widespread denervation with fibrillation potentials. Which of the following is the most likely unifying diagnosis?', '["FTD-ALS (Frontotemporal Dementia with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis) \u2014 a recognized overlap syndrome where behavioral/cognitive features of FTD co-occur with upper and lower motor neuron signs of ALS, most commonly associated with C9orf72 mutations", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease causing both cognitive decline and mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs", "Parkinson''''s disease \u2014 causing both cognitive decline and progressive motor weakness with fasciculations", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 prion disease causing both cognitive decline and motor neuron degeneration within weeks", "Alzheimer''''s disease with coincidental cervical myelopathy \u2014 the motor findings are unrelated to the cognitive decline"]'::jsonb, 0, '**FTD-ALS** is a well-recognized overlap syndrome in which features of **frontotemporal dementia** (behavioral changes, executive dysfunction, language impairment) co-occur with **amyotrophic lateral sclerosis** (upper motor neuron signs: hyperreflexia, spasticity, Babinski; AND lower motor neuron signs: muscle weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillation potentials on EMG). Key points: (1) **~15% of FTD patients** develop motor neuron disease; **~50% of ALS patients** develop some degree of cognitive/behavioral impairment; (2) The most common genetic cause is **C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion**; (3) When both FTD and ALS features are present, the prognosis is significantly worse (median survival 2-3 years) compared to either condition alone; (4) Pathologically, most FTD-ALS cases show **TDP-43** inclusions. EMG findings (widespread denervation, fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) confirm lower motor neuron involvement. Bulbar symptoms (dysphagia, dysarthria) are common and may necessitate early advance care planning and discussion of PEG tube placement. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female presents with concerns of gradual memory decline over the past year. The patient reports difficulty remembering recent conversations and occasionally forgetting appointments, but is still able to drive safely, manage complex finances independently, prepare meals, and complete all ADLs and IADLs without assistance. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA of **23/30** with impairment in **delayed recall** but normal performance in other domains. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and its prognostic significance?', '["Early-stage Alzheimer''''s dementia \u2014 functional independence is not relevant to the distinction between MCI and dementia", "Normal age-related memory decline \u2014 all elderly patients score 22-24/30 on MoCA and no further workup is needed", "Amnestic Mild Cognitive Impairment (aMCI) \u2014 objective memory impairment with preserved functional independence; carries the highest risk (~10-15% annual conversion rate) of progressing to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia", "Non-amnestic MCI \u2014 the memory impairment indicates this is the non-amnestic subtype with higher risk of converting to vascular dementia", "Pseudodementia \u2014 all memory complaints in elderly patients are attributable to depression until proven otherwise"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Amnestic MCI (aMCI)** is defined by: (1) **Subjective memory complaint** corroborated by an informant; (2) **Objective memory impairment** on cognitive testing (impaired delayed recall, as in this case); (3) **Preserved functional independence** — the patient can still manage ADLs (bathing, dressing, feeding) AND complex IADLs (finances, medications, driving, cooking) without assistance; (4) **NOT meeting criteria for dementia** (functional independence is the KEY distinguishing factor). aMCI carries the **highest risk of converting to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia**, with annual conversion rates of **~10-15%** (compared to 1-2% in cognitively normal elderly). Risk factors for conversion include: APOE ε4 carrier status, abnormal AD biomarkers (low CSF Aβ42, high p-tau, positive amyloid PET), and greater degree of hippocampal atrophy. Importantly, **cholinesterase inhibitors are NOT recommended** for MCI — they do not prevent or delay conversion to dementia. Management focuses on exercise, cognitive engagement, cardiovascular risk factor modification, and regular monitoring. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Petersen MCI Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old man presents with concerns of gradual memory decline over the past year. The patient reports difficulty remembering recent conversations and occasionally forgetting appointments, but is still able to drive safely, manage complex finances independently, prepare meals, and complete all ADLs and IADLs without assistance. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA of **23/30** with impairment in **delayed recall** but normal performance in other domains. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and its prognostic significance?', '["Early-stage Alzheimer''''s dementia \u2014 functional independence is not relevant to the distinction between MCI and dementia", "Amnestic Mild Cognitive Impairment (aMCI) \u2014 objective memory impairment with preserved functional independence; carries the highest risk (~10-15% annual conversion rate) of progressing to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia", "Pseudodementia \u2014 all memory complaints in elderly patients are attributable to depression until proven otherwise", "Non-amnestic MCI \u2014 the memory impairment indicates this is the non-amnestic subtype with higher risk of converting to vascular dementia", "Normal age-related memory decline \u2014 all elderly patients score 22-24/30 on MoCA and no further workup is needed"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Amnestic MCI (aMCI)** is defined by: (1) **Subjective memory complaint** corroborated by an informant; (2) **Objective memory impairment** on cognitive testing (impaired delayed recall, as in this case); (3) **Preserved functional independence** — the patient can still manage ADLs (bathing, dressing, feeding) AND complex IADLs (finances, medications, driving, cooking) without assistance; (4) **NOT meeting criteria for dementia** (functional independence is the KEY distinguishing factor). aMCI carries the **highest risk of converting to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia**, with annual conversion rates of **~10-15%** (compared to 1-2% in cognitively normal elderly). Risk factors for conversion include: APOE ε4 carrier status, abnormal AD biomarkers (low CSF Aβ42, high p-tau, positive amyloid PET), and greater degree of hippocampal atrophy. Importantly, **cholinesterase inhibitors are NOT recommended** for MCI — they do not prevent or delay conversion to dementia. Management focuses on exercise, cognitive engagement, cardiovascular risk factor modification, and regular monitoring. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Petersen MCI Criteria)'),
