    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old woman presents with concerns of gradual memory decline over the past year. The patient reports difficulty remembering recent conversations and occasionally forgetting appointments, but is still able to drive safely, manage complex finances independently, prepare meals, and complete all ADLs and IADLs without assistance. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA of **24/30** with impairment in **delayed recall** but normal performance in other domains. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and its prognostic significance?', '["Early-stage Alzheimer''''s dementia \u2014 functional independence is not relevant to the distinction between MCI and dementia", "Normal age-related memory decline \u2014 all elderly patients score 22-24/30 on MoCA and no further workup is needed", "Pseudodementia \u2014 all memory complaints in elderly patients are attributable to depression until proven otherwise", "Amnestic Mild Cognitive Impairment (aMCI) \u2014 objective memory impairment with preserved functional independence; carries the highest risk (~10-15% annual conversion rate) of progressing to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia", "Non-amnestic MCI \u2014 the memory impairment indicates this is the non-amnestic subtype with higher risk of converting to vascular dementia"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Amnestic MCI (aMCI)** is defined by: (1) **Subjective memory complaint** corroborated by an informant; (2) **Objective memory impairment** on cognitive testing (impaired delayed recall, as in this case); (3) **Preserved functional independence** — the patient can still manage ADLs (bathing, dressing, feeding) AND complex IADLs (finances, medications, driving, cooking) without assistance; (4) **NOT meeting criteria for dementia** (functional independence is the KEY distinguishing factor). aMCI carries the **highest risk of converting to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia**, with annual conversion rates of **~10-15%** (compared to 1-2% in cognitively normal elderly). Risk factors for conversion include: APOE ε4 carrier status, abnormal AD biomarkers (low CSF Aβ42, high p-tau, positive amyloid PET), and greater degree of hippocampal atrophy. Importantly, **cholinesterase inhibitors are NOT recommended** for MCI — they do not prevent or delay conversion to dementia. Management focuses on exercise, cognitive engagement, cardiovascular risk factor modification, and regular monitoring. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Petersen MCI Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female presents with concerns of gradual memory decline over the past year. The patient reports difficulty remembering recent conversations and occasionally forgetting appointments, but is still able to drive safely, manage complex finances independently, prepare meals, and complete all ADLs and IADLs without assistance. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA of **25/30** with impairment in **delayed recall** but normal performance in other domains. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and its prognostic significance?', '["Amnestic Mild Cognitive Impairment (aMCI) \u2014 objective memory impairment with preserved functional independence; carries the highest risk (~10-15% annual conversion rate) of progressing to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia", "Normal age-related memory decline \u2014 all elderly patients score 22-24/30 on MoCA and no further workup is needed", "Early-stage Alzheimer''''s dementia \u2014 functional independence is not relevant to the distinction between MCI and dementia", "Pseudodementia \u2014 all memory complaints in elderly patients are attributable to depression until proven otherwise", "Non-amnestic MCI \u2014 the memory impairment indicates this is the non-amnestic subtype with higher risk of converting to vascular dementia"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Amnestic MCI (aMCI)** is defined by: (1) **Subjective memory complaint** corroborated by an informant; (2) **Objective memory impairment** on cognitive testing (impaired delayed recall, as in this case); (3) **Preserved functional independence** — the patient can still manage ADLs (bathing, dressing, feeding) AND complex IADLs (finances, medications, driving, cooking) without assistance; (4) **NOT meeting criteria for dementia** (functional independence is the KEY distinguishing factor). aMCI carries the **highest risk of converting to Alzheimer''''s disease dementia**, with annual conversion rates of **~10-15%** (compared to 1-2% in cognitively normal elderly). Risk factors for conversion include: APOE ε4 carrier status, abnormal AD biomarkers (low CSF Aβ42, high p-tau, positive amyloid PET), and greater degree of hippocampal atrophy. Importantly, **cholinesterase inhibitors are NOT recommended** for MCI — they do not prevent or delay conversion to dementia. Management focuses on exercise, cognitive engagement, cardiovascular risk factor modification, and regular monitoring. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Petersen MCI Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old woman presents with difficulty planning complex tasks, organizing finances, and multitasking over the past year. Memory, language, and visuospatial function are intact on testing. The patient remains fully independent in all ADLs and IADLs but requires more effort than before for complex tasks. MoCA score is **22/30**, with deficits primarily in the **trail-making** and **abstraction** subtests. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what conditions is this subtype at risk of progressing to?', '["Behavioral variant FTD \u2014 executive dysfunction with preserved memory always indicates FTD regardless of functional status", "Amnestic MCI \u2014 the executive dysfunction indicates this is the amnestic subtype at risk for Alzheimer''''s disease", "Vascular dementia \u2014 executive dysfunction in the elderly is diagnostic of vascular dementia even without imaging evidence", "Non-amnestic MCI (executive dysfunction subtype) \u2014 objective impairment in executive function with preserved memory and functional independence; at higher risk of progressing to vascular dementia, DLB, or frontotemporal dementia rather than Alzheimer''''s disease", "ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) \u2014 new-onset executive dysfunction in elderly patients represents late-onset ADHD"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Non-amnestic MCI** is characterized by objective impairment in cognitive domains OTHER than memory — including **executive function** (planning, organization, multitasking, set-shifting), **language**, **attention**, or **visuospatial skills** — with **preserved functional independence**. This patient has the **executive dysfunction subtype** (impaired trail-making and abstraction, which test executive function). Non-amnestic MCI subtypes may progress to different dementias depending on the affected domain: (1) **Executive dysfunction** → higher risk of **vascular dementia** or **frontotemporal dementia**; (2) **Visuospatial impairment** → higher risk of **Dementia with Lewy Bodies**; (3) **Language impairment** → may progress to **Primary Progressive Aphasia** (FTD spectrum). This contrasts with **amnestic MCI**, which most commonly progresses to **Alzheimer''''s disease**. Not all MCI patients progress — some remain stable and ~15-20% may even revert to normal cognition. (Ref: Petersen MCI Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
