    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old man presents with difficulty planning complex tasks, organizing finances, and multitasking over the past year. Memory, language, and visuospatial function are intact on testing. The patient remains fully independent in all ADLs and IADLs but requires more effort than before for complex tasks. MoCA score is **22/30**, with deficits primarily in the **trail-making** and **abstraction** subtests. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what conditions is this subtype at risk of progressing to?', '["ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) \u2014 new-onset executive dysfunction in elderly patients represents late-onset ADHD", "Behavioral variant FTD \u2014 executive dysfunction with preserved memory always indicates FTD regardless of functional status", "Non-amnestic MCI (executive dysfunction subtype) \u2014 objective impairment in executive function with preserved memory and functional independence; at higher risk of progressing to vascular dementia, DLB, or frontotemporal dementia rather than Alzheimer''''s disease", "Amnestic MCI \u2014 the executive dysfunction indicates this is the amnestic subtype at risk for Alzheimer''''s disease", "Vascular dementia \u2014 executive dysfunction in the elderly is diagnostic of vascular dementia even without imaging evidence"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Non-amnestic MCI** is characterized by objective impairment in cognitive domains OTHER than memory — including **executive function** (planning, organization, multitasking, set-shifting), **language**, **attention**, or **visuospatial skills** — with **preserved functional independence**. This patient has the **executive dysfunction subtype** (impaired trail-making and abstraction, which test executive function). Non-amnestic MCI subtypes may progress to different dementias depending on the affected domain: (1) **Executive dysfunction** → higher risk of **vascular dementia** or **frontotemporal dementia**; (2) **Visuospatial impairment** → higher risk of **Dementia with Lewy Bodies**; (3) **Language impairment** → may progress to **Primary Progressive Aphasia** (FTD spectrum). This contrasts with **amnestic MCI**, which most commonly progresses to **Alzheimer''''s disease**. Not all MCI patients progress — some remain stable and ~15-20% may even revert to normal cognition. (Ref: Petersen MCI Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old woman is diagnosed with Mild Cognitive Impairment. The patient asks about strategies to reduce the risk of progressing to dementia. Which of the following interventions has the STRONGEST evidence for reducing dementia risk in MCI patients?', '["Daily high-dose donepezil (23 mg) \u2014 cholinesterase inhibitors reliably prevent MCI from converting to dementia in all clinical trials", "Strict carbohydrate-free ketogenic diet \u2014 eliminating all carbohydrates prevents amyloid plaque formation and reverses cognitive decline", "Daily high-dose vitamin E (2000 IU) supplementation \u2014 vitamin E has been proven to reverse MCI and restore normal cognition", "Daily completion of crossword puzzles alone, without any physical activity \u2014 isolated cognitive tasks are equally effective as exercise in preventing dementia conversion", "Regular aerobic physical exercise (e.g., 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking) \u2014 exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence for reducing dementia risk and slowing cognitive decline in MCI"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Among all interventions studied for MCI, **regular aerobic physical exercise** has the **strongest and most consistent evidence** for slowing cognitive decline and reducing dementia risk. The 2018 AAN practice guideline specifically recommends exercise (150 min/week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity) for MCI. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Exercise** (strongest evidence) — improves cerebral blood flow, neuroplasticity, BDNF levels, and cardiovascular health; (2) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** — controlling hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and obesity (these are modifiable risk factors for dementia); (3) **Mediterranean/MIND diet** — associated with reduced dementia risk in observational studies; (4) **Cognitive engagement** — social activities, learning new skills (complementary to exercise, not a substitute); (5) **Sleep optimization** — treating sleep apnea and insomnia. **NOT recommended**: Cholinesterase inhibitors (failed to show benefit in MCI trials), high-dose vitamin E (inconsistent evidence, potential cardiovascular risk), and ginkgo biloba (no proven benefit). (Ref: AAN MCI Practice Guideline 2018; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Lancet Commission on Dementia 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old man is diagnosed with Mild Cognitive Impairment. The patient asks about strategies to reduce the risk of progressing to dementia. Which of the following interventions has the STRONGEST evidence for reducing dementia risk in MCI patients?', '["Daily high-dose vitamin E (2000 IU) supplementation \u2014 vitamin E has been proven to reverse MCI and restore normal cognition", "Daily completion of crossword puzzles alone, without any physical activity \u2014 isolated cognitive tasks are equally effective as exercise in preventing dementia conversion", "Daily high-dose donepezil (23 mg) \u2014 cholinesterase inhibitors reliably prevent MCI from converting to dementia in all clinical trials", "Regular aerobic physical exercise (e.g., 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking) \u2014 exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence for reducing dementia risk and slowing cognitive decline in MCI", "Strict carbohydrate-free ketogenic diet \u2014 eliminating all carbohydrates prevents amyloid plaque formation and reverses cognitive decline"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Among all interventions studied for MCI, **regular aerobic physical exercise** has the **strongest and most consistent evidence** for slowing cognitive decline and reducing dementia risk. The 2018 AAN practice guideline specifically recommends exercise (150 min/week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity) for MCI. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Exercise** (strongest evidence) — improves cerebral blood flow, neuroplasticity, BDNF levels, and cardiovascular health; (2) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** — controlling hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and obesity (these are modifiable risk factors for dementia); (3) **Mediterranean/MIND diet** — associated with reduced dementia risk in observational studies; (4) **Cognitive engagement** — social activities, learning new skills (complementary to exercise, not a substitute); (5) **Sleep optimization** — treating sleep apnea and insomnia. **NOT recommended**: Cholinesterase inhibitors (failed to show benefit in MCI trials), high-dose vitamin E (inconsistent evidence, potential cardiovascular risk), and ginkgo biloba (no proven benefit). (Ref: AAN MCI Practice Guideline 2018; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Lancet Commission on Dementia 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old woman is diagnosed with Mild Cognitive Impairment. The patient asks about strategies to reduce the risk of progressing to dementia. Which of the following interventions has the STRONGEST evidence for reducing dementia risk in MCI patients?', '["Daily high-dose vitamin E (2000 IU) supplementation \u2014 vitamin E has been proven to reverse MCI and restore normal cognition", "Daily high-dose donepezil (23 mg) \u2014 cholinesterase inhibitors reliably prevent MCI from converting to dementia in all clinical trials", "Daily completion of crossword puzzles alone, without any physical activity \u2014 isolated cognitive tasks are equally effective as exercise in preventing dementia conversion", "Strict carbohydrate-free ketogenic diet \u2014 eliminating all carbohydrates prevents amyloid plaque formation and reverses cognitive decline", "Regular aerobic physical exercise (e.g., 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking) \u2014 exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence for reducing dementia risk and slowing cognitive decline in MCI"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Among all interventions studied for MCI, **regular aerobic physical exercise** has the **strongest and most consistent evidence** for slowing cognitive decline and reducing dementia risk. The 2018 AAN practice guideline specifically recommends exercise (150 min/week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity) for MCI. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Exercise** (strongest evidence) — improves cerebral blood flow, neuroplasticity, BDNF levels, and cardiovascular health; (2) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** — controlling hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and obesity (these are modifiable risk factors for dementia); (3) **Mediterranean/MIND diet** — associated with reduced dementia risk in observational studies; (4) **Cognitive engagement** — social activities, learning new skills (complementary to exercise, not a substitute); (5) **Sleep optimization** — treating sleep apnea and insomnia. **NOT recommended**: Cholinesterase inhibitors (failed to show benefit in MCI trials), high-dose vitamin E (inconsistent evidence, potential cardiovascular risk), and ginkgo biloba (no proven benefit). (Ref: AAN MCI Practice Guideline 2018; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Lancet Commission on Dementia 2020)'),
