    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old man is diagnosed with Mild Cognitive Impairment. The patient asks about strategies to reduce the risk of progressing to dementia. Which of the following interventions has the STRONGEST evidence for reducing dementia risk in MCI patients?', '["Daily high-dose vitamin E (2000 IU) supplementation \u2014 vitamin E has been proven to reverse MCI and restore normal cognition", "Regular aerobic physical exercise (e.g., 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking) \u2014 exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence for reducing dementia risk and slowing cognitive decline in MCI", "Strict carbohydrate-free ketogenic diet \u2014 eliminating all carbohydrates prevents amyloid plaque formation and reverses cognitive decline", "Daily high-dose donepezil (23 mg) \u2014 cholinesterase inhibitors reliably prevent MCI from converting to dementia in all clinical trials", "Daily completion of crossword puzzles alone, without any physical activity \u2014 isolated cognitive tasks are equally effective as exercise in preventing dementia conversion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Among all interventions studied for MCI, **regular aerobic physical exercise** has the **strongest and most consistent evidence** for slowing cognitive decline and reducing dementia risk. The 2018 AAN practice guideline specifically recommends exercise (150 min/week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity) for MCI. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Exercise** (strongest evidence) — improves cerebral blood flow, neuroplasticity, BDNF levels, and cardiovascular health; (2) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** — controlling hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and obesity (these are modifiable risk factors for dementia); (3) **Mediterranean/MIND diet** — associated with reduced dementia risk in observational studies; (4) **Cognitive engagement** — social activities, learning new skills (complementary to exercise, not a substitute); (5) **Sleep optimization** — treating sleep apnea and insomnia. **NOT recommended**: Cholinesterase inhibitors (failed to show benefit in MCI trials), high-dose vitamin E (inconsistent evidence, potential cardiovascular risk), and ginkgo biloba (no proven benefit). (Ref: AAN MCI Practice Guideline 2018; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Lancet Commission on Dementia 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old female is worried about memory lapses. During the evaluation, the patient occasionally forgets where she placed her car keys but always finds them eventually, occasionally takes longer to recall names of acquaintances, and processes new information slightly more slowly than before. All ADLs and IADLs are performed independently and efficiently. Cognitive testing shows a MoCA score of **27/30** with all domain scores within normal limits. Which of the following best characterizes this patient''''s cognitive status?', '["Subclinical vascular dementia \u2014 slowed processing speed always indicates subcortical white matter ischemia requiring urgent MRI", "Mild Cognitive Impairment \u2014 any memory complaint in an elderly patient meets criteria for MCI regardless of cognitive test scores", "Pseudodementia \u2014 the patient''''s concern about memory indicates underlying depression as the primary cause", "Normal age-associated cognitive changes \u2014 slower processing speed, occasional word-finding difficulty, and mild forgetfulness are expected in healthy aging and do NOT indicate MCI or dementia when cognitive testing is normal and function is fully preserved", "Early Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 misplacing keys is an early hallmark of Alzheimer''''s disease that requires immediate treatment with donepezil"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Normal age-associated cognitive changes** are common and should be distinguished from MCI and dementia. Normal aging involves: (1) **Slower processing speed** — takes longer to learn new information, which is the most consistent age-related cognitive change; (2) **Occasional word-finding difficulty** (''''tip of the tongue'''' phenomenon); (3) **Mild forgetfulness** — misplacing items but eventually finding them, forgetting names but recalling them later; (4) **Intact learning ability** — can learn new information, just takes more time and repetition. Critically, **cognitive testing is NORMAL** (MoCA ≥26 is generally considered normal) and **all functional abilities are fully preserved**. This contrasts with: **MCI** — objective impairment on cognitive testing (typically MoCA 18-25) with preserved functional independence; **Dementia** — objective cognitive impairment PLUS functional dependence (cannot manage ADLs or IADLs independently). Reassurance and education are appropriate; no pharmacotherapy is indicated. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
